        "Three chapters at the centre of the gospel — Matthew 5, 6, and 7 — together form the most influential single passage of moral teaching in Western literature. The Sermon opens with the Beatitudes: <em>blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven; blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted; blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.</em> The form of the macarism was familiar in the wisdom literature; what Matthew’s Jesus does with it is attach the blessing to the conditions worldly wisdom does not bless. The poor, the mourning, the meek, the merciful, the persecuted — the conditions that count in the kingdom Jesus is announcing are not the conditions a society organised by wealth and power and security would name as blessed.",
